hints.content:
Notice the numerator is missing a $1^{\text{st}}$ degree term. Let's add it as $0x$.

$\begin{array}{r}
x\phantom{{}-28}
\\
x+5|\overline{x^2+0x-28}
\\
\mathllap{-(}\underline{x^2+5x\phantom{-28}\rlap )}
\\
{}-5x-28
\end{array}$

options.explanation:
We start by asking, "what can we multiply by $x+5$ to get $x^2+k$ (no $x$-term) where $k$ is an integer?"

The answer is $x-5$ (remember the difference of squares pattern):

$\blueD{(x+5)(x-5)=x^2-25}$

Now we rewrite the numerator and split the quotient:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{x^2-28}{x+5}&=\dfrac{(\blueD{x^2-25})-3}{x+5}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-25}}{x+5}-\dfrac{3}{x+5}
\end{align}$

Now we use the fact that $\blueD{(x+5)(x-5)=x^2-25}$ to rewrite the numerator of the first quotient and cancel common factors:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{\blueD{x^2-25}}{x+5}-\dfrac{3}{x+5}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{\blueD{(x+5)}}\blueD{(x-5)}}{\cancel{x+5}}-\dfrac{3}{x+5}
\\\\
&=x-5-\dfrac{3}{x+5}
\end{align}$
